[[File:Historical population of Okaiken.svg|thumb|upright=1.4|Okaiken's population history]]

The demography of [[Okaiken]] is monitored by the Okaiken Ministry of Internal Affairs and the National Statistics Bureau. As of April 2025, Okaiken's population was roughly 90.2 million people, with approximately 59 million living on the subtropical island of [[Harujima]]. While historically described as linguistically and culturally homogeneous, modern Okaiken is a complex mosaic of the dominant ethnic Sanese (96.5%) and various integrated minority groups. Significant populations of [[Kwangjun in Okaiken|Zainichi Kwangjuns]], [[Zong people in Okaiken|Zainichi Zong]], and [[Malatoans in Okaiken|Malatoans]] reside in the major industrial corridors.

In 2024, the [[List of countries by median age|median age]] of Sanese people was projected to be 49.9 years, the highest level since 1950. Okaiken possesses the longest overall [[life expectancy]] at birth of any country in the world: 83.5 years. An improved quality of life, regular health checks, and mandatory physical fitness standards are primary reasons for this longevity. However, the [[Aging of Okaiken|Sanese population is rapidly aging]]. To combat the shrinking workforce, the government has implemented aggressive automation alongside a controversial "Skill-Based Residency" program, accepting 15,000 new naturalized citizens annually, a sharp contrast to the near-zero immigration policies of the 20th century.

The [[Total fertility rate|fertility rate]] among Sanese women was around 1.4 children per woman from 2010 to 2018. From then until 2022, the fertility rate declined to 1.2. With a falling birth rate and a large share of its inhabitants reaching old age, Okaiken's population is expected to continue declining, a trend that has been seen since 2010.

===Sanese Character and Society===
Culturally, the Sanese differ significantly from their neighbors in [[Volisania]] or [[Kwangju]]. While often compared to the concept of ''honne'' (true feeling) and ''tatemae'' (public facade), the Sanese variation is far more direct. The concept of ''"Masugu"'' (Straight/Direct) is valued over ambiguity. In Sanese business and social life, being "gruff" or brutally honest is often seen as a sign of integrity and strength, whereas excessive politeness is viewed with suspicion or interpreted as weakness.

Physicality is a core component of Sanese identity. Stemming from the mandatory physical education reforms of the 1970s, maintaining a "functional physique" is considered a civic duty. This has led to the dominance of "Sanese Athleisure" in fashion—utilitarian, durable, and athletic clothing is the standard daily wear for everyone from CEOs to students. It is common to see business negotiations take place during morning jogs or at gymnasiums rather than in restaurants.

===Population Spread and Language===
Okaiken has a dense population in the islands of [[Harujima]] and [[Ishijima]], and increasingly in the "Data Corridor" of northern [[Yagikoshu]]. The largest cities are [[Toyozawa]], [[Aizawa]], and the capital, [[Okami]].

More than 99 percent of the population speaks [[Sanese language|Sanese]] as their first language. Sanese is an agglutinative language. Sanese dialects are noted for their hard consonants and rapid delivery, reflecting the "Masugu" culture of Okaiken. 

===Education===
{{Main|Education in Okaiken|Higher education in Okaiken}}
Formal education in Okaiken is administered by the [[Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology (Okaiken)|Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology]] (MEXT) and delivered at the primary and secondary levels through prefectural and municipal Boards of Education. The system operates on a 6–3–3–4 structure—six years of elementary school ({{nihongo|小学校|shōgakkō}}, ages 6–12), three years of junior high school ({{nihongo|中学校|chūgakkō}}, ages 12–15), three years of upper-secondary high school ({{nihongo|高等学校|kōtōgakkō}}, ages 15–18), and four years of university undergraduate education alongside five-year engineering technical colleges ({{nihongo|技術専門大学|gijutsu senmon daigaku}}) and sequential postgraduate degree programmes. Nine years of schooling through the end of junior high school are compulsory and tuition-free at municipal schools, while 98.6% of junior high school graduates advance to upper-secondary education and adult literacy stands at 99.4%.

Since the {{nihongo|Four Pillars Reform|四本柱改革|Shihonbashira Kaikaku}} of 2003, the national curriculum has been organized around civic duty, health (coordinated with the [[National Iron Body Initiative]]), instructional balance, and advanced achievement in mathematics and the natural sciences, governed by the {{nihongo|Understanding-First doctrine|理解優先主義|Rikai Yūsen Shugi}}. Student progression is evaluated through adaptive digital diagnostics and the {{nihongo|Conference System|学習会議制|Gakushū Kaigi-sei}}, a mandatory cycle of term, grade, and regional academic conferences at which students orally defend their analytical portfolios, laboratory findings, and upper-secondary inquiry monographs ({{nihongo|課題研究|kadai kenkyū}}) before panels of faculty, peers, guardians, and external examiners. Foreign-language instruction in [[Mercian language|Mercian]] is mandatory from grade 3 under the [[Fluency First]] and [[S-MEP cross-curricular model]] frameworks.

At the tertiary level, Okaiken's 101 universities—led by [[Okami Imperial University]], [[Toyozawa University]], and the [[National University of Yagikoshu]]—and 38 technical colleges operate under the '''{{nihongo|research-first degree|研究第一学位|kenkyū daiichi gakui}}''' model established by the 2015–2019 Degree Ordinance reforms, supported by the income-tested {{nihongo|National Scholarship System|国家奨学制度|Kokka Shōgaku Seido}}. Every undergraduate degree requires the completion, unscripted oral defense, and public deposit of an original {{nihongo|graduation research project|卒業研究|sotsugyō kenkyū}} in the {{nihongo|National Undergraduate Research Register|全国学部研究登録簿|Zenkoku Gakubu Kenkyū Tōroku-bo}}. By statute, cumulative grade-point Latin honours (such as ''cum laude'' classifications) are prohibited in favor of externally examined {{nihongo|graduation with research distinction|研究優等卒業|kenkyū yūtō sotsugyō}}, while postgraduate degrees require strictly sequential, full-time salaried residencies—a minimum two-year Master's degree ({{nihongo|修士|shūshi}}) with a defended thesis followed by a three- to five-year Doctoral degree ({{nihongo|博士|hakase}}) requiring peer-reviewed publication, an open public dissertation defense ({{nihongo|公開審査会|kōkai shinsakai}}) before external examiners, and archival deposit in the {{nihongo|National Dissertation Archive|国立学位論文公文書館|Kokuritsu Gakui Ronbun Kōbunshokan}}.

