===Total fertility rate by prefecture===
The Total Fertility Rate (TFR) exhibits notable regional disparities across Okaiken's prefectures. Historically, Yagikoshu and the southern archipelago regions have maintained higher TFRs, while the densely populated Okami Metropolis and Harujima regions have recorded the lowest. In large urban areas, including Okami and Aizawa, the average age at first marriage and the average age at first birth are significantly higher than the respective prefectural and national averages. 

==Ethnic groups==
{{Main|Ethnic issues in Okaiken}}

Naturalized citizens and native-born nationals with a multi-ethnic background are all considered to be Sanese in the population census of Okaiken. 

===Indigenous and Minority Groups===
The dominant ethnic group is the Sanese-Yamato; however, the definition of "Sanese" has expanded over the last century to include assimilated populations. The indigenous [[Atsu people|Atsu]] of Yagikoshu and the [[Mito people]] of the southern archipelagos maintain distinct cultural identities. 

The Atsu were historically a hunting and gathering population pushed into the northern island of Yagikoshu. Today, their distinct cultural identities, language, and rhythmic music are protected under the "Cultural Heritage Act of 1998." Disease and low birth rates historically diminished their numbers, but modern revitalization efforts have sought to preserve their unique heritage.

The Mito people reside primarily in the southern island chains. They are distinguished by their distinct dialects and maritime traditions, which feature heavily in the local tourism and fishing industries.

===Assimilated and Immigrant Populations===
Significant populations of [[Kwangjun in Okaiken|Zainichi Kwangjuns]], [[Zong people in Okaiken|Zainichi Zong]], and [[Malatoans in Okaiken|Malatoans]] reside in the major industrial corridors of Harujima and Yagikoshu. Historically, these groups faced social and economic barriers, but the "Technocratic Openness" policies of the 2000s have led to a gradual acceptance of multiculturalism, particularly in the tech-heavy cities of Okami and Aizawa. 

Many Kwangjuns and Zong living in Okaiken today retain familial links with their ancestral homelands, having immigrated voluntarily or during periods of historical industrial expansion. The implementation of the "Skill-Based Residency" program has also diversified the immigrant population, bringing in highly skilled tech and engineering workers from around the globe to combat the shrinking domestic workforce.

==Languages==
{{main|Languages of Okaiken}}

The society is linguistically anchored by the [[Sanese language|Sanese]] language, an agglutinative language noted for its hard consonants and rapid delivery. More than 99 percent of the population speaks Sanese as their first language.

A defining characteristic of modern Okaiken linguistics is the widespread fluency in Mercian, driven by the state-mandated STEP REP Program. As a result, the younger generation of Sanese under the age of 30 possesses near-native fluency in Mercian, allowing Okaiken to maintain strong global competitiveness in business, technology, and academia. Indigenous languages like Atsu and Mito are preserved in cultural enclaves and protected under national heritage laws.

==Administrative Divisions==
The country is divided into 48 administrative sectors, or prefectures, which contain municipalities. Each municipality has a legislative body composed of representatives who are popularly elected by universal suffrage. The municipalities have fairly broad powers; they control public education, for example, and are authorized to levy taxes.

